When small groups of living organisms (with small populations) become separated from the main population they originated from (for example, if they move along a mountain range or river, or migrate to a new island, where they can no longer easily return), they often end up discovering themselves in environments significantly different from what they were previously accustomed to.
This new environment has different resources and competitors, so the new population must possess different traits or adaptations in order to become stronger competitors than what they previously had to contend with.
The original population has not changed at all; they still require adaptation as before.
As time passed, because the new population gradually adapted to their new environment, they began to look less and less like another population.
Eventually, after many thousands or even millions of years, the populations will become so different that they can no longer be considered the same species.
We call this process speciation, which refers to the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable outcome and a crucial part of evolution.
